k**  \nLet’s check 2.624673:  \nWe can use natural log:  \n\\(\\ln(421.381) = \\ln(4.21381) + \\ln(100)\\) — wait, better:  \n\\(\\log_{10}(421.381) = \\ln(421.381) / \\ln(10)\\).\n\nCompute \\(\\ln(421.381)\\):  \n\\(\\ln(421.381) = \\ln(4.21381 \\times 100) = \\ln(4.21381) + \\ln(100)\\)  \n\\(\\ln(100) = 4.605170186\\)  \nNow \\(\\ln(4.21381)\\):  \nWe know \\(\\ln(4.2) \\approx 1.435084525\\), and 4.21381 is slightly more.  \nRatio 4.21381 / 4.2 ≈ 1.0032881, ln ≈ 0.0032827.  \nSo ln(4.21381) ≈ 1.435084525 + 0.0032827 ≈ 1.438367225.\n\nAdd ln(100): total ≈ 1.438367225 + 4.605170186 = 6.043537411.\n\nDivide by ln(10) ≈ 2.302585093:  \n6.043537411 / 2.302585093 ≈ 2.624669 (approx).\n\nMatches our earlier estimate.\n\n---\n\n**Step 6 – More precision**  \nLet’s refine:  \nWe can do high-precision:  \nUse \\(\\log_{10}(421.381) = \\log_{10}(4.21381) + 2\\).  \n\\(\\log_{10}(4.21381)\\):  \nWe know \\(\\log_{10}(4) \\approx 0.6020599913\\), \\(\\log_{10}(4.21381/4) = \\log_{10}(1.0534525)\\).  \n\\(\\ln(1.0534525) \\approx 0.052076\\) (small x expansion: x=0.0534525, ln ≈ 0.0534525 - 0.001428 + 0.0000509 ≈ 0.0520754).  \nDivide by ln(10): 0.0520754 / 2.302585093 ≈ 0.022616.  \nAdd to log10(4): 0.6020599913 + 0.022616 ≈ 0.624676.  \nThen +2 → 2.624676.\n\nSo more precisely ≈ 2.624676.\n\n---\n\n**Step 7 – Final rounding**  \nWe can give 2.624676... to at least 10 significant digits.  \nLet’s take a couple more digits: using better ln(1.0534525):  \nx = 0.0534525  \nx^2/2 = 0.00142858  \nx^3/3 ≈ 0.00005091  \nx^4/4 ≈ 0.00000204  \nln ≈ 0.0534525 - 0.00142858 + 0.00005091 - 0.00000204 ≈ 0.05207279.  \nDivide by 2.302585093 ≈ 0.02261536.  \nAdd 0.6020599913 → 0.62467535.  \nAdd 2 → 2.62467535.\n\nSo to 10 sig figs: 2.624675350.\n\n---\n\n**Final answer**:\n<<<2.624675350>>>",
